theory DualSE_PinLockstep
begin

/* ===========================================================================
   PQ-Signer — three-way PIN-attempt lockstep, stateful model (Tamarin)
   ===========================================================================

   Companion to the ProVerif secrecy model (contracts/verification/proverif/).
   ProVerif proves message-level secrecy/authentication; this proves the
   STATEFUL reconcile property it cannot reason about (counter resets +
   reachable-state safety).

   Mechanism (threat-model.md §6.2 / secure/src/nsc/mod.rs::reconcile_pin_attempts):
   three failed-attempt counters — MCU page-124, OPTIGA F1E1, SE050 UserID —
   move in LOCKSTEP (every wrong PIN bumps all three). At boot, reconcile()
   WIPES the device if the three counters DISAGREE (tamper signal). An attacker
   can RESET one counter (OPTIGA via the S-1 glitch / SE050 delete-recreate /
   a TZ-bypass MCU-erase) to try to win back attempts.

   !! MODEL <-> CODE FIDELITY CAVEAT (2026-07-02, finding PM-1) !!
   This model wipes on ANY disagreement (a SYMMETRIC reconcile). The DEPLOYED
   reconcile_pin_attempts (secure/src/nsc/mod.rs) is DIRECTIONAL:
   `tamper = (se_count > mcu) || se_split` (se_count = max(optiga, se050);
   se_split = optiga != se050 when both readable). It catches STRICTLY LESS than
   this model proves:
     - a coordinated reset of BOTH SE silicon counters (optiga=se050=0) yields
       se_count=0, se_split=false, 0 > mcu = false  =>  NO WIPE, whereas this
       model proves that trace wipes. So the "partial reset never survives"
       lemma below is an IDEALIZED-SPEC result, NOT a faithful statement about
       the deployed directional code.
     - in the shipping config the SE050 counter read returns None (policy
       denial), so se_split is dead and even a single OPTIGA reset is undetected
       by reconcile (though still bounded by the primary controls).
   These reconcile gaps are a DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH cross-check only: the primary
   controls (per-SE silicon lockout + the FI-hardened MCU page-124 pre-commit in
   nsc::gated_unlock) still bound the attacker to <= MAX_ATTEMPTS, so there is no
   fund-drain — the harm is that this model + the README below OVERSTATE what the
   deployed reconcile catches. Re-modeling the directional predicate over ordered
   counters (so the both-SE reset appears as a NEW residual) is the tracked
   follow-up (docs/work-todo.md).

   PROVEN (see lemmas): a surviving boot is reachable ONLY when either NO
   counter was reset, or ALL THREE were. Equivalently, a PARTIAL reset (one or
   two of the three) can NEVER survive a boot — the un-reset counter(s) desync
   the reset one and reconcile wipes. A single-side reset (S-1 alone, SE050
   delete alone, MCU erase alone) is always caught. The all-three reset is the
   documented residual that the hardware-monotonic OPTIGA counter (work-todo
   #24 / ship-blocker S-3) closes by making Reset_OPT impossible — dropping the
   attacker from "reset 2 of 3" to "reset 1 of 3", which is caught here.

   ABSTRACTIONS (stated, not discovered):
   * Each counter's value is abstracted to a STATUS: 'fresh' (in lockstep) or
     'zeroed' (attacker-reset). This is faithful for the RECONCILE property:
     lockstep counters always agree, a reset desyncs one. It deliberately drops
     the exact "<= 10" per-counter silicon cap (enforced inside each SE; out of
     this model's frame) — what is proven is that the reconcile DEFENDS that cap
     against resets, not the cap value itself.
   * The boot rule is TERMINAL (one boot per trace) so the state space is finite
     and the safety lemmas are decidable. reconcile runs at EVERY boot, so the
     per-boot property proven here is exactly the per-boot guarantee; it does
     not model a device continuing across multiple boots in one trace.
   =========================================================================== */

/* provisioning: all three counters synced ('fresh'); device Live. */
rule Provision:
    [ Fr(~sid) ]
  --[ Provision(~sid) ]->
    [ MCU(~sid, 'fresh'), OPT(~sid, 'fresh'), SE(~sid, 'fresh'), Live(~sid) ]

/* attacker resets ONE counter to 'zeroed' (powered-off; no Live needed). */
rule Reset_MCU: [ MCU(sid, st) ] --[ Reset(sid, 'MCU') ]-> [ MCU(sid, 'zeroed') ]
rule Reset_OPT: [ OPT(sid, st) ] --[ Reset(sid, 'OPT') ]-> [ OPT(sid, 'zeroed') ]
rule Reset_SE:  [ SE(sid, st)  ] --[ Reset(sid, 'SE')  ]-> [ SE(sid, 'zeroed') ]

/* boot reconcile (TERMINAL). Outcome split by the agreed status so the safety
   lemmas avoid a disjunctive conclusion:
     - all three 'fresh'  => survive (SyncedFresh)
     - all three 'zeroed' => survive (SyncedZero)
     - any disagreement   => Wipe. */
rule Boot_Fresh:
    [ MCU(sid, 'fresh'), OPT(sid, 'fresh'), SE(sid, 'fresh'), Live(sid) ]
  --[ Boot(sid), SyncedFresh(sid) ]->
    [ Survived(sid) ]

rule Boot_Zero:
    [ MCU(sid, 'zeroed'), OPT(sid, 'zeroed'), SE(sid, 'zeroed'), Live(sid) ]
  --[ Boot(sid), SyncedZero(sid) ]->
    [ Survived(sid) ]

rule Boot_Wipe:
    [ MCU(sid, m), OPT(sid, o), SE(sid, s), Live(sid) ]
  --[ Boot(sid), Wipe(sid), Differ(m, o, s) ]->
    [ Wiped(sid) ]

/* Boot_Wipe fires only on genuine disagreement. */
restriction differ:
    "All m o s #i. Differ(m, o, s) @ i ==> (not(m = o)) | (not(o = s)) | (not(m = s))"

/* ===========================================================================
   LEMMAS  (all four verified by `tamarin-prover --prove`)
   =========================================================================== */

/* ANTI-VACUITY: a provisioned device can boot and SURVIVE without a wipe. If
   this fails the model is trivially always-wipe and the safety lemmas below
   are meaningless. */
lemma honest_boot_possible:
  exists-trace
    "Ex sid #b. SyncedFresh(sid) @ b & not (Ex #w. Wipe(sid) @ w)"

/* CORE (A): a surviving FRESH boot implies NO counter was EVER reset before it.
   (The un-reset counters can only stay 'fresh'; any reset would desync them.) */
lemma fresh_synced_means_no_reset:
  all-traces
    "All sid #b.
        SyncedFresh(sid) @ b
      ==> not (Ex c #r. Reset(sid, c) @ r & r < b)"

/* CORE (B): a surviving ZERO boot implies ALL THREE counters were reset before
   it. Together with (A): a surviving boot is reachable ONLY via no-reset or
   all-three-reset, so a PARTIAL (one/two) reset can never survive — the
   three-way reconcile catches every single-side reset. */
lemma zero_synced_means_all_reset:
  all-traces
    "All sid #b.
        SyncedZero(sid) @ b
      ==> ( Ex #rm #ro #rs.
                Reset(sid, 'MCU') @ rm & rm < b
              & Reset(sid, 'OPT') @ ro & ro < b
              & Reset(sid, 'SE')  @ rs & rs < b )"

/* RESIDUAL (documented limitation): resetting ALL THREE counters together does
   survive a boot — reconcile cannot see an all-zero agreement. This is the gap
   the hardware-monotonic OPTIGA counter closes (it removes Reset_OPT). */
lemma full_reset_bypass:
  exists-trace
    "Ex sid #rm #ro #rs #b.
        Reset(sid, 'MCU') @ rm & rm < b
      & Reset(sid, 'OPT') @ ro & ro < b
      & Reset(sid, 'SE')  @ rs & rs < b
      & SyncedZero(sid) @ b
      & not (Ex #w. Wipe(sid) @ w)"

end
